Commit:     Mark Shank <mark.shank@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Wed Oct 21 15:50:17 2026 -0400

    Score long V4.1 decode index rows from bf16 keys
    
    The indexer's keys are FP4 values times a power-of-two scale, and every kernel that writes them rounds them to bf16, so a bf16 copy holds them exactly in half the space of the f32 key cache. Those kernels now also write a bf16 copy, and once there are 8192 or more index keys, decode scores them from the copy. The scoring math and its order are unchanged, so the results are bit-identical. Restoring a saved session rebuilds the copy, and if any restored key isn't exact in bf16, scoring uses the f32 keys. This is Metal only.
    
    DS4_METAL_DISABLE_V41_INDEX_BF16=1 skips the copy entirely.

